Hello! I'd be happy to help you understand some Prima.js models. Prima.js is a simple and lightweight library for building web applications using the Model-View-Controller (MVC) architectural pattern. Here are some examples of Prima.js models:

1. **Basic Model:**

```javascript
import { Model } from 'prima';

class User extends Model {
  static schema = {
    name: { type: 'string' },
    email: { type: 'string', unique: true },
    age: { type: 'number' },
  };
}

export default User;
```

In this example, we have a `User` model with `name`, `email`, and `age` fields. The `schema` property defines the structure and validation rules for the model.

2. **Model with Associations:**

```javascript
import { Model, hasMany } from 'prima';

class Post extends Model {
  static schema = {
    title: { type: 'string' },
    content: { type: 'text' },
  };

  comments = hasMany('Comment', 'post_id');
}

class Comment extends Model {
  static schema = {
    content: { type: 'text' },
    post_id: { type: 'number', ref: 'Post.id' },
  };

  post = belongsTo('Post', 'post_id');
}

export { Post, Comment };
```

Here, we have `Post` and `Comment` models with a one-to-many association. A `Post` has many `Comments`, and each `Comment` belongs to a `Post`. The `hasMany` and `belongsTo` methods are used to define these associations.

3. **Model with Custom Methods:**

```javascript
import { Model } from 'prima';

class User extends Model {
  static schema = {
    name: { type: 'string' },
    email: { type: 'string', unique: true },
    age: { type: 'number' },
  };

  fullName() {
    return `${this.firstName} ${this.lastName}`;
  }
}

export default User;
```

In this example, the `User` model has a custom method `fullName()` that returns the full name of the user by concatenating the `firstName` and `lastName` fields.

4. **Model with Validations:**

```javascript
import { Model } from 'prima';

class User extends Model {
  static schema = {
    name: { type: 'string', required: true },
    email: { type: 'string', unique: true, required: true },
    age: { type: 'number', min: 18, max: 100 },
  };

  validate(data) {
    if (data.age < 18) {
      return 'You must be at least 18 years old';
    }
  }
}

export default User;
```

Here, the `User` model has validations for the `name`, `email`, and `age` fields. Additionally, a custom validation is added to ensure that the user's age is at least 18.

These examples demonstrate some of the basic features of Prima.js models. You can build upon these examples to create more complex models tailored to your application's needs. For more information, you can refer to the official Prima.js documentation: https://primajs.com/docs/models/

