In C and C++, when you assign one struct variable to another struct variable of the same type, no type transformation occurs. The assignment is a simple copy of the values from one struct to the other, member by member. This is known as a shallow copy, where the members of the struct are copied by value.

However, if the struct contains pointers or references to other data, a shallow copy may not be sufficient, as it only copies the memory addresses and not the actual data being pointed to. In such cases, you might need to perform a deep copy, where you allocate new memory for the destination struct's pointers and copy the data from the source struct's pointers to the new memory locations.

Here's an example to illustrate the difference between shallow and deep copies:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <cstring>

struct MyStruct {
    int x;
    char* str;
};

void shallow_copy(MyStruct& dest, const MyStruct& src) {
    dest = src; // Shallow copy: only copies the memory addresses of 'str'
}

void deep_copy(MyStruct& dest, const MyStruct& src) {
    dest.x = src.x;
    if (src.str) {
        dest.str = new char[strlen(src.str) + 1];
        strcpy(dest.str, src.str);
    } else {
        dest.str = nullptr;
    }
}

int main() {
    MyStruct src;
    src.x = 42;
    src.str = new char[5];
    strcpy(src.str, "test");

    MyStruct dest;

    // Shallow copy
    shallow_copy(dest, src);
    std::cout << "Shallow copy: " << dest.x << ", " << dest.str << std::endl;

    // Deep copy
    deep_copy(dest, src);
    std::cout << "Deep copy: " << dest.x << ", " << dest.str << std::endl;

    // Free memory
    delete[] src.str;
    delete[] dest.str;

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, the shallow copy only copies the memory address of `str`, while the deep copy allocates new memory and copies the actual data being pointed to.

